Writer: a .docx with many comments no longer parses comments.xml once per comment.

Every comment reference created a sub-document for comments.xml and parsed the whole part; only the
<w:comment> with the wanted id was forwarded (OOXMLFastContextHandlerXNote). N comments therefore cost
O(N^2) XML parsing on the main thread. Measured with the 1.0.2 batch5 build on Linux (4 CPUs) on a
2000-paragraph document with 4000 tracked changes: 2 comments 5.8 s to load, 500 comments 9.2 s, 1000
comments 19.6 s, 2000 comments 71 s, during which the window was frozen (65 s); a 10 MB reviewed
document did not open within 15 minutes.

comments.xml is now read once, on the first comment, and cut into its top-level elements. Each comment
parses a small document made of the original prolog and root start tag (namespace declarations), the
<w:comment> elements with its id in document order, and the root end tag, so the import is linear.
The sub-document is otherwise the same (OOXMLStreamImpl::setDocumentInput replaces only the bytes that
are parsed). Anything unexpected keeps the old full parse: UTF-16 or NUL bytes, a DTD with an internal
subset, a top-level element other than a comment or without an id, unbalanced markup.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).
Test: CppunitTest_sw_writerfilter_ooxml testCommentsEachGetOwnText (22 comments, each with the text of
its own element); the existing comment import/export tests; tests/unit/test_large_office_open_patches.py.
